// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["x-a"] = {"qs": [["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "Which return measure removes the effect of the timing of investor cash flows and is preferred for judging a fund manager?", ["Money-weighted return", "Simple absolute return", "Dividend yield", "Time-weighted return"], 3, "Time-weighted return links sub-period returns, so it is not distorted by when investors add or withdraw money, which the manager does not control. Money-weighted return (IRR) reflects the investor's own timing."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "Dynamic asset allocation differs from strategic allocation because it:", ["Never changes once set at the start", "Invests only in fixed deposits", "Is used only by government pension funds", "Changes the mix based on market conditions"], 3, "Dynamic allocation shifts between equity and debt in response to valuations or other signals. Strategic allocation is a long-term target mix maintained by periodic rebalancing."], ["Time Value of Money", "A home loan of ₹20 lakh is taken at 9% per annum for 15 years with monthly EMIs. The EMI is closest to:", ["₹15,000", "₹20,285", "₹26,111", "₹11,111"], 1, "EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1) with r = 0.75% and n = 180, giving about ₹20,285. ₹11,111 ignores interest and ₹15,000 is only the first month's interest."], ["Equity Investment", "A share trades at ₹300 and its book value per share is ₹120. What is its price-to-book ratio?", ["0.4 times", "3.0 times", "2.5 times", "1.8 times"], 2, "P/B = market price ÷ book value per share = 300 ÷ 120 = 2.5. A ratio of 0.4 would result from dividing book value by price, which inverts the formula."], ["Debt Investment", "A credit rating of 'AAA' on a bond indicates:", ["A guarantee by the government of repayment", "That the bond will never fall in price", "The highest degree of safety on timely servicing", "That the issuer is a public sector company"], 2, "AAA denotes the highest safety regarding timely payment of interest and principal, in the rating agency's opinion. It is not a guarantee, and the bond's price can still change with interest rates."], ["Behavioural Finance", "Investors rush to buy a theme because everyone around them is buying it. This behaviour is:", ["Anchoring", "Herding", "Framing", "Endowment effect"], 1, "Herding is following the crowd instead of independent analysis, which can inflate bubbles. It often leads to buying near peaks and selling near troughs."], ["Mutual Funds", "An investor invests ₹6,000 each month for three months at NAVs of ₹20, ₹25 and ₹30. What is his average cost per unit?", ["₹25.00", "About ₹26.10", "About ₹24.32", "₹20.00"], 2, "Units bought = 300 + 240 + 200 = 740. Average cost = 18,000 ÷ 740 ≈ ₹24.32. This is below the simple average NAV of ₹25, illustrating rupee cost averaging."], ["Derivatives", "For a retail client seeking capital protection, an adviser considering derivatives should first check:", ["Which derivative offers the highest leverage", "The latest tips on social media", "Whether the client understands and can bear the risks", "Whether the broker offers free trades"], 2, "Suitability requires that the client understands the product and can bear potential losses. Leverage, tips or brokerage offers are not appropriate grounds for recommending derivatives."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Which instrument is issued by banks to raise short-term funds and is a money market instrument?", ["Commercial paper", "Certificate of deposit", "Treasury bill", "Equity share"], 1, "Certificates of deposit are issued by banks and certain financial institutions. Commercial paper is issued by companies, treasury bills by the government through the RBI, and equity shares are capital market instruments."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "A fund returned 12% when the risk-free rate was 6%. Its downside deviation was 8%. What is its Sortino ratio?", ["0.75", "1.50", "0.50", "2.00"], 0, "Sortino ratio = (portfolio return − risk-free rate) ÷ downside deviation = (12 − 6) ÷ 8 = 0.75. Unlike Sharpe, it penalises only downside volatility."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "In a portfolio management service, the client's securities are held:", ["In the portfolio manager's proprietary account", "In a pooled account like a mutual fund", "In the client's own demat account", "With the stock exchange as collateral"], 2, "PMS portfolios are held individually in the client's name, giving full transparency and individual ownership. Mutual funds pool money and investors hold units, not the underlying securities."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "An insurance agent who gives advice only on insurance products sold by him needs to register as an investment adviser?", ["No, such advice is exempt", "Yes, under all circumstances", "Yes, if he sells more than ten policies a year", "Only if he advises on term insurance"], 0, "IA Regulations exempt insurance agents and brokers who offer advice only on insurance products regulated by IRDAI. Advising on securities would require registration."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser shares a client's portfolio details with a property developer without consent. This breaches:", ["Diversification norms", "Client confidentiality", "Asset allocation policy", "Rupee cost averaging"], 1, "Client information must be kept confidential and used only for advisory purposes unless consent is given or law requires disclosure. Sharing it with a developer is a serious breach."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "An adviser recommends a product mainly because it pays her a higher fee from the issuer, though a cheaper suitable option exists. This breaches the principle of:", ["Acting in the client's best interest", "Diversification across asset classes", "Rupee cost averaging", "Tax efficiency of investment products"], 0, "A fiduciary must place the client's interest ahead of her own. Choosing a product for personal gain when a better suitable option exists is a conflict of interest and a breach of fiduciary duty."], ["Behavioural Finance", "A client prefers a plan described as having a '90% chance of meeting the goal' over one with a '10% chance of failing', though they are identical. This is:", ["Mental accounting", "Overconfidence", "Framing", "Disposition effect"], 2, "Framing means decisions change with how identical information is presented. Advisers should present information neutrally and in several ways so the client's choice is not driven by wording."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "Why must a mid-cap fund be compared with a mid-cap index rather than the Nifty 50?", ["The benchmark must match the fund's universe", "The Nifty 50 is not a valid index", "Mid-cap indices always give higher returns", "SEBI bans comparison with large-cap indices"], 0, "A benchmark should reflect the fund's investment universe and risk. Comparing a mid-cap fund with a large-cap index mixes style effects with manager skill."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "In-person verification during KYC can now be done through:", ["A phone call without video", "An email with a scanned signature only", "A newspaper advertisement", "Video-based verification"], 3, "SEBI permits video-based in-person verification by intermediaries, using secure processes, as an alternative to physical meetings. Phone calls or emails alone do not meet IPV norms."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "Calendar rebalancing means a portfolio is rebalanced:", ["At fixed intervals, such as yearly", "Only when the client asks for a new product", "Whenever the market moves by 1% in a day", "Never, to save transaction costs"], 0, "Calendar rebalancing resets the portfolio to target weights at regular intervals. Threshold rebalancing instead triggers when weights drift beyond set bands."], ["Derivatives", "An interest rate swap typically involves:", ["Buying shares in exchange for bonds", "Converting rupees into dollars at a fixed rate permanently", "Exchanging fixed-rate for floating-rate payments", "Swapping one mutual fund scheme for another"], 2, "In a plain interest rate swap, one party pays a fixed rate and the other a floating rate on a notional amount, with only net interest exchanged. It helps manage interest rate exposure."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "Which fee arrangement is permitted for a SEBI-registered investment adviser?", ["Commission from mutual fund houses", "A share of insurance premiums", "A fixed fee or a fee based on assets", "Brokerage on trades the client places"], 2, "SEBI allows advisers to charge either a fixed fee or a fee linked to assets under advice, within prescribed limits, from the client only. Commissions and brokerage are not permitted."], ["Debt Investment", "Which bond will be most sensitive to a change in interest rates, other things equal?", ["A 20-year zero-coupon bond", "A 2-year bond with a high coupon", "A 91-day treasury bill", "A 5-year floating rate bond reset every quarter"], 0, "Price sensitivity rises with maturity and falls with coupon size. A long zero-coupon bond has the highest duration, so its price moves most. Floating rate bonds reset coupons and have low interest rate sensitivity."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "Two funds have equal returns. Fund X has a higher standard deviation than Fund Y. Based on the Sharpe ratio:", ["Fund X has better risk-adjusted return", "Fund Y has better risk-adjusted return", "Both have identical risk-adjusted returns", "The Sharpe ratio cannot compare them"], 1, "With the same excess return, the fund with lower standard deviation has a higher Sharpe ratio. Fund Y delivered the same return for less total risk."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "Investment advisers must conduct risk profiling to:", ["Decide which product pays the highest fee", "Ensure advice suits the client's risk", "Fulfil a tax filing requirement", "Rank clients by income for marketing"], 1, "Risk profiling assesses a client's capacity and willingness to take risk, so advice can be matched to it. It is a core suitability requirement, not a marketing or tax tool."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "Why is a periodic review an essential step of the financial planning process?", ["SEBI requires advisers to change the portfolio every month", "Reviews allow the adviser to earn additional commissions", "A plan once made can never be modified, so reviews only confirm it", "Client circumstances and markets change over time"], 3, "Income, goals, family situation, tax laws and market conditions change, so a plan must be monitored and updated. Reviews are not about churning the portfolio or earning commissions."], ["Debt Investment", "The difference between the yield on a AA-rated corporate bond and a government security of similar maturity mainly reflects:", ["The inflation rate in the economy", "The face value of the two bonds", "The tax deducted on government securities", "Credit and liquidity risk"], 3, "The credit spread compensates investors for the corporate issuer's default risk and usually lower liquidity. Both bonds face the same inflation environment, so inflation does not explain the spread."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A client has total assets of ₹1 crore and total liabilities of ₹40 lakh. What is the solvency ratio (net worth ÷ total assets)?", ["40%", "60%", "250%", "167%"], 1, "Net worth = ₹1 crore − ₹40 lakh = ₹60 lakh. Solvency ratio = 60 lakh ÷ 1 crore = 60%. 40% is the liabilities-to-assets ratio, the complement of solvency."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "Adding an asset with negative correlation to an existing portfolio is likely to:", ["Increase overall portfolio volatility sharply", "Reduce overall portfolio volatility", "Have no effect on portfolio risk", "Guarantee a higher return"], 1, "Negatively correlated assets tend to move in opposite directions, smoothing portfolio returns and lowering volatility. Diversification does not guarantee higher returns."], ["Behavioural Finance", "Loss aversion means that investors:", ["Feel losses more than equal gains", "Prefer losses to gains of the same size", "Are indifferent between gains and losses", "Avoid all equity investments by law"], 0, "Prospect theory shows a loss hurts roughly twice as much as an equal gain pleases. This can make investors overly cautious or reluctant to realise losses."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Treasury bills in India are currently issued with which maturities?", ["30, 60 and 90 days", "1, 3 and 5 years", "7, 14 and 28 days", "91, 182 and 364 days"], 3, "The Government of India issues treasury bills of 91, 182 and 364 days through RBI auctions. They are issued at a discount and redeemed at face value."], ["Derivatives", "An investor sells index futures against a diversified portfolio. If the market rises sharply, the futures position will:", ["Lose money, offsetting portfolio gains", "Gain money, adding to portfolio gains", "Be unaffected by market movement", "Be automatically closed by SEBI"], 0, "A short futures hedge loses when the market rises, offsetting gains on the portfolio. Hedging reduces both downside and upside, locking in the current value."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "Fair treatment of clients requires that an adviser:", ["Does not favour some clients over others", "Gives the best ideas only to the largest clients", "Charges different fees without disclosure", "Allocates scarce opportunities to family members first"], 0, "Fair dealing means treating all clients equitably in advice and allocation, with transparent fees. Favouring large clients or family members breaches this duty."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "An InvIT mainly holds:", ["Residential flats rented to individuals", "Gold bars held in bank vaults", "Infrastructure assets such as roads and power lines", "Shares of listed IT companies"], 2, "Infrastructure Investment Trusts own revenue-generating infrastructure assets like toll roads, transmission lines and pipelines. REITs hold real estate, and gold or shares are held by other vehicles."], ["Mutual Funds", "Under SEBI's scheme categorisation, a multi cap fund must invest at least:", ["65% in large-cap stocks only", "25% each in large, mid and small caps", "80% in a single sector", "50% in debt and 50% in equity"], 1, "SEBI requires multi cap funds to hold at least 25% each in large-, mid- and small-cap stocks, ensuring true diversification across market caps. Flexi cap funds have no such minimums."], ["Time Value of Money", "An investment grew from ₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh in 6 years. What is its compound annual growth rate?", ["About 12.2%", "About 16.7%", "About 10.0%", "About 14.5%"], 0, "CAGR = (2 ÷ 1)^(1/6) − 1 ≈ 12.25%. 16.7% is the simple average (100% ÷ 6), which ignores compounding and overstates the annual growth rate."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "A fund beat its benchmark by 2% a year with a tracking error of 4%. What is its information ratio?", ["2.0", "8.0", "0.5", "0.08"], 2, "Information ratio = active return ÷ tracking error = 2 ÷ 4 = 0.5. It measures how consistently a manager adds return over the benchmark per unit of active risk."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "A portfolio manager reports performance to clients. SEBI requires PMS performance to be reported using:", ["Only the return in the best month", "The return before any fees and expenses", "A simple average of yearly returns", "A time-weighted rate of return"], 3, "SEBI requires PMS returns to be computed on a time-weighted basis, net of fees and expenses, and compared with a benchmark, allowing fair comparison across managers."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "A client has three goals: emergency fund, child's education in 12 years and a foreign holiday next year. Which is generally prioritised first?", ["The foreign holiday next year", "The child's education in 12 years", "The emergency fund", "All goals should be funded equally from the start"], 2, "An emergency fund protects all other goals from being derailed by sudden expenses or income loss, so it is usually the first priority. Essential long-term goals come next, and discretionary goals after that."], ["Derivatives", "A client with no shares sells call options to earn premium. What is his risk?", ["Loss limited to the premium received", "Potentially unlimited losses if the stock rises", "No risk, since he receives money upfront", "Loss limited to the strike price"], 1, "A naked call writer must deliver at the strike if exercised, so losses grow without limit as the stock rises. The premium received is his maximum gain, not his maximum loss."], ["Securities Market Segments", "Through RBI Retail Direct, an individual investor can:", ["Trade equity options directly with the RBI", "Buy unlisted shares of private companies", "Open a gilt account and buy government securities", "Invest in foreign stocks without any limit"], 2, "RBI Retail Direct lets individuals open a Retail Direct Gilt account with the RBI to buy G-secs in primary auctions and trade on NDS-OM. It does not cover equity options, unlisted shares or foreign stocks."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "A Category II AIF that invests in unlisted companies is best described as:", ["A liquid mutual fund", "A private equity fund", "A government bond fund", "An index exchange traded fund"], 1, "Private equity and private debt funds are typical Category II AIFs investing in unlisted companies or instruments. Liquid funds, G-sec funds and ETFs are mutual fund products."], ["Securities Market Segments", "In which segment would a trader buy USD/INR futures?", ["Equity cash segment", "Currency derivatives segment", "Debt segment", "SME platform"], 1, "USD/INR futures and options trade in the currency derivatives segment of recognised exchanges. The equity cash segment is for shares, the debt segment for bonds and the SME platform for small company shares."], ["Equity Investment", "A company splits its shares from face value ₹10 to ₹5. An investor held 200 shares at ₹800. Ignoring market movement, after the split she holds:", ["200 shares at about ₹400", "400 shares at about ₹800", "100 shares at about ₹1,600", "400 shares at about ₹400"], 3, "A 2-for-1 split doubles the share count and halves the price, leaving total value unchanged at ₹1,60,000. It does not change her proportionate ownership."], ["Debt Investment", "If market yields fall after a client buys a fixed-coupon bond, the bond's market price will:", ["Fall", "Rise", "Remain unchanged", "Become zero"], 1, "Bond prices move inversely to yields. When yields fall, existing bonds with higher coupons become more valuable, so their prices rise."], ["Debt Investment", "A 3-year bond with face value ₹100 pays a 7% annual coupon. If the market yield for such bonds is 8%, its price is closest to:", ["₹100.00", "₹97.42", "₹102.67", "₹93.00"], 1, "Price = present value of coupons and principal at 8%: 7/1.08 + 7/1.08² + 107/1.08³ ≈ ₹97.42. When yield exceeds coupon, the bond trades at a discount; ₹102.67 is the price at a 6% yield."], ["Debt Investment", "A conservative client wants regular income with minimal credit risk for five years. Which option best fits?", ["Unrated debentures of a small company", "Perpetual bonds of a weak bank", "A sector equity fund", "Government securities of about five years"], 3, "G-secs carry sovereign backing with negligible credit risk and pay regular coupons. Unrated or perpetual bonds of weak issuers carry significant credit risk, and equity funds do not offer stable income."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "A flat bought for ₹80 lakh earns annual rent of ₹2.4 lakh. What is its gross rental yield?", ["3%", "30%", "0.3%", "6%"], 0, "Gross rental yield = annual rent ÷ property value = 2.4 lakh ÷ 80 lakh = 3%. Residential yields in Indian cities are often low, so returns depend heavily on capital appreciation."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "Compared with a mutual fund, a PMS generally offers:", ["More customisation at a higher minimum", "Lower minimum investment and pooled ownership", "Guaranteed returns approved by SEBI", "Daily NAV published on AMFI's website"], 0, "PMS offers a personalised portfolio with individual holdings, but has a high minimum ticket and often performance-linked fees. Mutual funds are pooled, have low minimums and publish daily NAVs."], ["Securities Market Segments", "An investor buys shares of a listed company from another investor on the exchange. This trade takes place in the:", ["Secondary market", "Primary market", "Money market", "Over-the-counter forward market"], 0, "Trading in already-issued securities between investors happens in the secondary market. The primary market is where issuers sell new securities to raise capital."], ["Derivatives", "Why is futures trading considered leveraged?", ["Only a margin is paid for the full exposure", "The exchange lends shares to every trader free of cost", "Futures prices cannot move more than 1% a day", "The full contract value must be paid upfront in cash"], 0, "With futures, a margin that is a fraction of contract value gives exposure to the full value, so small price moves cause large gains or losses relative to the money deposited."], ["Time Value of Money", "If the discount rate used to value a future goal increases, the present value of that goal will:", ["Fall", "Rise", "Stay the same", "Double"], 0, "Present value = future value ÷ (1 + r)^n. A higher discount rate increases the denominator, so the present value falls. This is why the assumed return strongly affects required savings."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "Why is gold often included in a diversified portfolio?", ["It pays a high regular dividend", "It is guaranteed to beat inflation every year", "Its price never falls in rupee terms", "It tends to have low correlation with equities"], 3, "Gold often behaves differently from equities, especially in crises, so a modest allocation can reduce portfolio volatility. It pays no income on its own and its price can fall."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "Category I AIFs include funds that invest in:", ["Listed derivatives with high leverage", "Only government securities", "Only gold and silver", "Start-ups, SMEs and infrastructure"], 3, "Category I AIFs include venture capital, angel, SME and infrastructure funds that the government considers economically or socially desirable. Leveraged trading strategies fall under Category III."], ["Equity Investment", "Which of the following is the main source of long-term return for equity investors?", ["Fixed interest paid by the company every quarter", "Guaranteed repayment of capital on a maturity date", "Growth in company earnings and dividends", "Interest credited by the stock exchange"], 2, "Equity returns come from dividends and price appreciation driven by earnings growth. Equity has no fixed interest or maturity, and exchanges do not pay interest to shareholders."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A personal cash flow statement differs from a net worth statement because it shows:", ["Assets and liabilities at a point in time", "Inflows and outflows over a period", "Only the market value of investments held", "Only the tax payable by the client"], 1, "A cash flow statement records income and expenses over a period, showing the surplus available. The net worth statement is a snapshot of assets minus liabilities on a given date."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "Who regulates and supervises registered investment advisers on behalf of SEBI through a recognised body?", ["AMFI", "NSDL", "BSE Administration and Supervision Ltd", "The Insurance Ombudsman"], 2, "SEBI has recognised BSE Administration and Supervision Limited (BASL), a BSE subsidiary, as the body to administer and supervise investment advisers. AMFI is a mutual fund industry body."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "A client's risk profile suggests 50% equity, but she wants 90% equity after hearing friends' stories. The adviser should:", ["Immediately raise equity to 90% to keep her happy", "Refuse to advise her any further", "Explain the risks and stick to a suitable allocation", "Move everything into a single hot stock"], 2, "Advisers must recommend suitable allocations and educate clients on risks. Changing the allocation purely due to peer influence would breach suitability; refusing to engage helps no one."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "In a repo transaction, a bank:", ["Sells securities and agrees to buy them back later", "Buys shares permanently from the RBI", "Issues new equity to the public", "Lends money without any collateral"], 0, "In a repo, the borrower sells securities with an agreement to repurchase them at a set price, making it a collateralised loan. The difference in prices is the interest, called the repo rate."], ["Securities Market Segments", "A company's first-ever offer of shares to the public, followed by listing, is called:", ["An initial public offer", "A follow-on public offer", "A rights issue", "A buyback offer"], 0, "An IPO is the first sale of shares to the public by an unlisted company, after which it lists on an exchange. An FPO is by a listed company, and rights issues go only to existing holders."], ["Debt Investment", "An upward-sloping yield curve means that:", ["Longer maturities have higher yields", "Short-term yields are higher than long-term yields", "All maturities carry the same yield", "Bond prices always rise over time"], 0, "A normal or upward-sloping yield curve shows higher yields for longer maturities, reflecting term premium and expectations. An inverted curve has short-term yields above long-term yields."], ["Equity Investment", "An investor prefers companies trading at low P/E and P/B ratios relative to their fundamentals. Her investment style is:", ["Growth investing", "Value investing", "Momentum investing", "Index investing"], 1, "Value investors seek stocks priced below their intrinsic worth, often indicated by low valuation multiples. Growth investors pay higher multiples for fast earnings growth, and momentum investors follow price trends."], ["Behavioural Finance", "A trader believes his stock picks are far better than average and trades very frequently. This reflects:", ["Overconfidence", "Mental accounting", "Loss aversion", "Home bias"], 0, "Overconfident investors overrate their knowledge and skill, leading to excessive trading and under-diversification, which tends to reduce net returns after costs."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "SEBI requires REITs to invest most of their assets in:", ["Undeveloped agricultural land", "Listed equity shares of any sector", "Completed, revenue-generating properties", "Foreign currency deposits"], 2, "SEBI regulations require the bulk of a REIT's value (at least 80%) to be in completed and rent-generating properties, limiting development risk. This supports stable distributions."], ["Securities Market Segments", "Why do exchanges maintain separate trading segments for equity, derivatives, currency and debt?", ["To ensure that investors can trade only one product type in their life", "Because each segment is owned by a different government ministry", "So that prices in one segment are never linked to another", "Each has its own contracts and risk rules"], 3, "Different instruments need different contract specifications, margins and settlement processes, so exchanges organise them into segments. Investors can trade in several segments, and prices across segments are closely linked."], ["Derivatives", "A client holds shares at ₹800 and buys a put with strike ₹760 for ₹15. If the share falls to ₹650 at expiry, his net loss per share including premium is:", ["₹150", "₹55", "₹40", "₹165"], 1, "The put limits the sale price to ₹760, so the stock loss is 800 − 760 = ₹40, plus the ₹15 premium = ₹55. Without the put, the loss would have been ₹150."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "A client complains to an adviser. Within what broad expectation should the adviser respond?", ["Promptly, within prescribed timelines", "Only when SEBI asks the adviser to respond", "After the client's next annual review", "Never, if the adviser thinks the complaint is wrong"], 0, "Advisers must have a grievance redressal mechanism and resolve complaints within SEBI's prescribed timelines. Unresolved complaints can be escalated by the client to SCORES and ODR."], ["Time Value of Money", "₹5 lakh is invested for 3 years at 8% per annum compounded quarterly. Its maturity value is closest to:", ["₹6,29,856", "₹6,20,000", "₹6,34,121", "₹6,40,000"], 2, "Quarterly rate 2% for 12 quarters: 5,00,000 × 1.02^12 ≈ ₹6,34,121. Annual compounding gives ₹6,29,856 and simple interest gives ₹6,20,000, both lower because interest compounds less often."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "Which client is most suited to a higher equity allocation?", ["A 75-year-old retiree living off portfolio income", "A client needing the money for a house in one year", "A client with no emergency fund and unstable income", "A 30-year-old with stable income and long goals"], 3, "A long horizon and stable income increase capacity to bear equity volatility. Short horizons, reliance on portfolio income and lack of buffers argue for lower equity."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A client has ₹2 lakh surplus. She has a car loan at 9%, a personal loan at 15% and a home loan at 8.5% with tax benefits. Which should she prepay first?", ["The home loan at 8.5%", "The car loan at 9%", "The personal loan at 15%", "Split the money equally across all three loans"], 2, "Prepaying the highest-cost debt gives the greatest guaranteed saving. The personal loan at 15% is costliest; the home loan is cheapest after tax benefits, so it should be prepaid last."], ["Mutual Funds", "A scheme's NAV is ₹50 and it has an exit load of 1% on redemptions within a year. An investor redeems within the year. What is the redemption price per unit?", ["₹50.50", "₹50.00", "₹49.00", "₹49.50"], 3, "Exit load is deducted from NAV: 50 × (1 − 0.01) = ₹49.50. ₹50.50 wrongly adds the load, which would apply to an entry load, now not charged by mutual funds."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "Which of the following is generally considered 'good debt'?", ["A credit card balance used to fund a holiday", "A personal loan taken to buy the latest smartphone", "An education loan for a skill that raises income", "A loan taken to speculate in futures and options"], 2, "Good debt finances assets or capabilities that are likely to grow in value or raise earning power. Consumption debt and borrowing to speculate do not create such value and often carry high cost."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "In a non-discretionary PMS, investment decisions are made:", ["By the manager only with client approval", "Entirely by the portfolio manager without consulting the client", "By SEBI on behalf of the client", "Automatically by an index-tracking algorithm"], 0, "In a non-discretionary PMS, the manager recommends and executes trades only after the client approves them. In a discretionary PMS, the manager decides on the client's behalf."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "For a well-diversified portfolio, which risk-adjusted measure uses beta as the measure of risk?", ["Sharpe ratio", "Treynor ratio", "Sortino ratio", "Information ratio"], 1, "The Treynor ratio divides excess return by beta, focusing on systematic risk, which is appropriate when unsystematic risk has been diversified away. Sharpe uses total standard deviation."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A client's liquid assets are ₹2,40,000 and monthly expenses are ₹80,000. His liquidity ratio (in months of expenses) is:", ["3 months", "2 months", "6 months", "0.33 months"], 0, "Liquidity ratio = liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses = 2,40,000 ÷ 80,000 = 3 months. It shows how long the client can meet expenses from liquid funds if income stops."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser holds shares of a company she is about to recommend to clients. The ethical approach is to:", ["Recommend it without mentioning her holding", "Buy more shares before recommending it", "Sell her shares just after clients buy", "Disclose her holding to clients"], 3, "Personal holdings in recommended securities are a conflict that must be disclosed, and trading around recommendations is improper. Clients need to know of any interest that may affect advice."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "A basic services demat account (BSDA) is meant for:", ["Institutional investors only", "Foreign portfolio investors", "Companies issuing shares", "Small investors with low holdings"], 3, "BSDA offers lower or nil annual maintenance charges to individuals with small holdings below a threshold, encouraging financial inclusion. Other accounts carry normal charges."], ["Equity Investment", "A company's net profit is ₹50 crore and it has 10 crore shares outstanding. What is its earnings per share?", ["₹5", "₹50", "₹0.50", "₹10"], 0, "EPS = net profit available to equity shareholders ÷ number of shares = 50 crore ÷ 10 crore = ₹5. The other figures misplace the decimal or confuse profit with share count."], ["Mutual Funds", "Equity linked savings schemes have a lock-in period of:", ["1 year", "5 years", "No lock-in at all", "3 years"], 3, "ELSS units are locked in for three years from the date of each investment, including each SIP instalment. They carry the shortest lock-in among tax-saving investment options under the old regime."], ["Equity Investment", "A shareholder bought a share at ₹400, received a dividend of ₹10 and sold it after one year at ₹440. What was his total return?", ["12.5%", "10.0%", "2.5%", "11.4%"], 0, "Total return = (price gain + dividend) ÷ purchase price = (40 + 10) ÷ 400 = 12.5%. 10% counts only the price gain and 2.5% only the dividend yield."], ["Mutual Funds", "The riskometer on a mutual fund scheme is meant to show:", ["The guaranteed return of the scheme", "The fund manager's personal rating", "The scheme's level of risk to investors", "The number of investors in the scheme"], 2, "The riskometer grades a scheme's risk from low to very high based on its portfolio and is reviewed monthly. It does not indicate returns or fund manager quality."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Commercial paper is typically issued by:", ["The central government to fund long-term deficits", "Highly rated corporates for short-term funds", "Retail investors to borrow from banks", "Stock exchanges to finance trading members"], 1, "Commercial paper is an unsecured short-term promissory note issued by creditworthy companies and certain financial institutions to meet working capital needs. Governments use T-bills and dated securities."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser receives a gift voucher from a fund house whose schemes she recommends. What should she do?", ["Accept it quietly as a normal business gift", "Recommend that fund house's schemes more often", "Decline it or disclose it as a conflict", "Share the voucher with the client to balance it"], 2, "Benefits from product providers create conflicts that can bias advice. The adviser should avoid such benefits or at least disclose them; increasing recommendations would worsen the breach."], ["Behavioural Finance", "Which adviser action best helps clients avoid behavioural mistakes during a market crash?", ["Switching the whole portfolio to cash immediately", "Revisiting the goal-based plan", "Recommending a new hot sector fund", "Avoiding any contact until markets recover"], 1, "Reminding clients of their long-term goals, allocation and past recoveries reduces panic selling. Knee-jerk moves or silence tend to worsen outcomes."], ["Mutual Funds", "Who holds the assets of a mutual fund scheme in safe custody?", ["The custodian", "The asset management company", "The sponsor", "The registrar and transfer agent"], 0, "The custodian, registered with SEBI, holds the scheme's securities. The AMC manages the money, the trustees oversee the AMC, and the RTA maintains unitholder records."], ["Mutual Funds", "A client wants to move money from a liquid fund into an equity fund in equal monthly instalments. Which facility suits this?", ["Systematic transfer plan", "Systematic withdrawal plan", "Dividend reinvestment option", "New fund offer"], 0, "An STP periodically transfers a fixed amount from one scheme to another of the same fund house, spreading equity entry over time. An SWP pays money out to the investor."], ["Debt Investment", "A bondholder receives coupons and must reinvest them at lower rates because interest rates have fallen. This is:", ["Credit risk", "Default risk", "Reinvestment risk", "Liquidity risk"], 2, "Reinvestment risk is the risk that intermediate cash flows will be reinvested at lower rates than expected, reducing realised returns. It rises when rates fall, the opposite of price risk."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "Showing a client only the best-performing period of a recommended fund, while hiding weaker periods, is:", ["Acceptable marketing practice", "Required by SEBI for clarity", "Misleading and unethical", "Fine if the client is a high-net-worth investor"], 2, "Selective presentation of performance misrepresents the product. Advisers must present balanced, fair information covering appropriate periods, risks and benchmarks."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "Before giving advice, a registered investment adviser must enter into:", ["A written agreement with the client", "An agreement with the fund house", "An arrangement with a stock broker", "A contract with the stock exchange"], 0, "IA Regulations require a client agreement covering terms, fees, scope and disclosures before advisory services begin. Arrangements with product providers would create conflicts."], ["Behavioural Finance", "A client refuses to sell a stock until it returns to the price he paid, ₹500, although its prospects have changed. This shows:", ["Herding", "Home bias", "Anchoring", "Overconfidence"], 2, "Anchoring is fixation on a reference point, here the purchase price, which is irrelevant to the stock's future value. Decisions should be based on current prospects."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Since the merger of the Forward Markets Commission with SEBI in 2015, commodity derivatives exchanges are regulated by:", ["RBI", "Ministry of Agriculture", "Competition Commission of India", "SEBI"], 3, "The FMC was merged into SEBI in September 2015, bringing commodity derivatives under SEBI's regulation alongside securities markets. RBI regulates money and forex markets, not commodity exchanges."], ["Securities Market Segments", "A small and medium enterprise wants to list with lighter eligibility norms than the main board. Where can it list?", ["The currency derivatives segment", "The commodity derivatives segment", "The negotiated dealing system of the RBI", "The SME platform of a stock exchange"], 3, "Exchanges run dedicated SME platforms (such as NSE Emerge and BSE SME) with separate eligibility and disclosure norms for smaller companies. Derivatives segments and NDS are for trading other instruments."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "Tracking error of an index fund measures:", ["The fund's expense ratio", "The index's average return", "The number of stocks in the index", "Volatility of the fund's return gap"], 3, "Tracking error is the standard deviation of the difference between fund and index returns, showing how consistently the fund replicates its benchmark. Lower is better for an index fund."], ["Behavioural Finance", "A client reads only news that supports his view that a stock will rise and ignores negative reports. This is:", ["Recency bias", "Anchoring", "Herding", "Confirmation bias"], 3, "Confirmation bias is seeking information that confirms existing beliefs and ignoring contrary evidence. Advisers can counter it by presenting balanced analysis."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "Which document is mandatory for most securities market transactions in India?", ["Passport", "Voter ID", "PAN", "Driving licence"], 2, "PAN is the key identifier for securities market transactions, with limited exemptions. Other documents may serve as address proof but are not mandatory identifiers."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "Gold ETFs in India are backed by:", ["Shares of gold mining companies only", "Bank fixed deposits", "Government securities", "Physical gold of specified purity"], 3, "Gold ETFs hold physical gold of prescribed purity, and their NAV tracks domestic gold prices. Funds investing in mining companies are equity funds, not gold ETFs."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser realises she lacks expertise in a client's complex cross-border tax issue. She should:", ["Give a confident answer to keep the client", "Ignore the issue in the plan", "Copy advice from an online forum", "Refer the client to a qualified expert"], 3, "Professional competence means knowing one's limits and seeking expert help where needed. Guessing or ignoring material issues can harm the client."], ["Time Value of Money", "Using the rule of 72, roughly how many years will an investment take to double at 9% per annum?", ["9 years", "6 years", "12 years", "8 years"], 3, "The rule of 72 estimates doubling time as 72 ÷ interest rate = 72 ÷ 9 = 8 years. The exact figure at 9% annual compounding is about 8.04 years, so the approximation is close."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "A client says his goal is 'to be rich some day'. What should the adviser do first to make this goal usable in a plan?", ["Recommend a small-cap equity fund straight away", "Help him define it in amount and time terms", "Ignore it and focus only on his tax-saving needs for the current year", "Ask him to choose a product from a list of popular schemes"], 1, "A planning goal must be specific, measurable and time-bound, so the adviser converts a vague wish into a target amount and date. Only then can the required savings and asset allocation be worked out."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "A mutual fund unitholder submits a redemption request. The AMC must normally pay the money within:", ["Ten working days", "Three working days", "One calendar month", "Fifteen working days"], 1, "SEBI and AMFI norms require redemption proceeds to be paid within three working days for most schemes, with interest for delays. Some exceptional cases allow longer timelines."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "Which piece of information is most important to collect before preparing a client's asset allocation?", ["The brand of car he plans to buy next year", "His goals, time horizon and risk profile", "The names of funds his friends have bought recently", "His preferred stock exchange for trading shares"], 1, "Asset allocation depends on what the money is for, when it is needed and how much risk the client can and will take. Peer choices and trivial preferences do not drive allocation."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "Why are PMS and AIF products generally suitable only for wealthier, experienced investors?", ["They are banned for residents of India", "They do not allow any investment in equity", "They need large tickets and carry higher risk", "They are guaranteed by the government"], 2, "High minimum investments, concentrated or complex strategies and limited liquidity make these products suitable mainly for investors with large portfolios and the ability to bear losses."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "A registered investment adviser wants to also distribute mutual funds to the same client and earn commissions. Under SEBI's framework this is:", ["Allowed if the adviser waives her advisory fee", "Allowed for clients with small portfolios", "Allowed if disclosed only after the sale", "Not allowed, as advice and distribution are segregated"], 3, "SEBI requires client-level segregation of advisory and distribution so that an adviser does not earn commissions from products she recommends. Waiving fees does not cure the conflict."], ["Ethics, Conflicts & Grievance Redressal", "If an investor's complaint is not resolved through SCORES, a further option is:", ["The Banking Ombudsman", "SEBI's online dispute resolution platform", "The Income Tax department", "The Competition Commission of India"], 1, "SEBI's ODR platform offers online conciliation and arbitration for unresolved securities market disputes. The banking ombudsman, tax department and CCI do not handle such disputes."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "Which statement best distinguishes an investment adviser from a mutual fund distributor?", ["The adviser can sell any product and earn commission from each fund house", "The distributor must charge a fixed fee directly to the client", "The adviser is paid by the client for advice", "Both are paid in the same way and have identical obligations"], 2, "A SEBI-registered investment adviser is remunerated only by the client and must act in the client's interest. A distributor is paid commissions by product manufacturers, which creates a different set of incentives."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A family's essential monthly expenses are ₹60,000. If the adviser recommends an emergency fund of six months' expenses, how much should it be?", ["₹60,000", "₹7,20,000", "₹1,80,000", "₹3,60,000"], 3, "Emergency fund = monthly essential expenses × months of cover = 60,000 × 6 = ₹3,60,000. ₹7,20,000 would be twelve months and ₹1,80,000 only three months."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Which regulator oversees the National Pension System?", ["IRDAI", "SEBI", "PFRDA", "NABARD"], 2, "The Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority regulates NPS and pension funds. IRDAI regulates insurance, SEBI regulates securities markets and NABARD is a development bank for agriculture."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "A client wants gold exposure without storage worries and with exchange liquidity. Which product fits best?", ["A gold exchange traded fund", "Gold jewellery from a local shop", "Gold coins kept in a home locker", "A gold loan from an NBFC"], 0, "Gold ETFs are held in demat form, avoid making charges and storage risk, and can be traded on exchanges. A gold loan is borrowing against gold, not an investment."], ["Behavioural Finance", "A client treats her bonus as 'fun money' and spends it on speculative trades while keeping her salary savings very conservative. This is:", ["Loss aversion", "Recency bias", "Status quo bias", "Mental accounting"], 3, "Mental accounting is treating money differently depending on its source or label, although all rupees are interchangeable. A unified portfolio view leads to better decisions."], ["PMS, AIF & Other Managed Portfolios", "Which AIF category can use leverage and employ complex trading strategies, including hedge funds?", ["Category I", "Category III", "Category II", "None, AIFs cannot use leverage"], 1, "Category III AIFs, such as hedge funds, may use leverage and complex strategies subject to limits. Category I invests in socially or economically desirable areas, and Category II includes private equity and debt funds without leverage for investment."], ["Time Value of Money", "A deposit earns 10% per annum while inflation is 6%. What is the real rate of return, using the exact formula?", ["Exactly 4.00%", "About 16.6%", "About 3.77%", "About 1.67%"], 2, "Real return = (1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.10 ÷ 1.06 − 1 ≈ 3.77%. Simply subtracting 6% from 10% gives 4%, which slightly overstates the real return."], ["Equity Investment", "Under SEBI's categorisation, mid-cap companies are those ranked:", ["1st to 100th by full market capitalisation", "251st onwards by full market capitalisation", "1st to 50th by free-float market capitalisation", "101st to 250th by full market capitalisation"], 3, "SEBI defines large caps as the top 100 companies, mid caps as 101st to 250th and small caps as 251st onwards, ranked by full market capitalisation using AMFI's periodic list."], ["Mutual Funds", "An arbitrage fund earns returns mainly from:", ["Long-term bets on small-cap stocks", "Price gaps between cash and futures markets", "Investing in foreign real estate", "Lending to unrated companies"], 1, "Arbitrage funds buy in the cash market and sell in futures (or vice versa) to capture the spread, giving low-risk, money-market-like returns while being treated as equity-oriented for tax."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Which statement about the capital market is correct?", ["It deals in long-term instruments like shares and bonds", "It deals only in instruments maturing within one year", "It is regulated mainly by IRDAI", "It does not include any secondary market trading"], 0, "The capital market provides long-term funds through equity and debt securities and includes both primary issues and secondary trading. Instruments under one year belong to the money market."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "An adviser's recommended scheme can be bought by the client through:", ["Regular plans that pay the adviser commission", "Any plan, as long as the client signs a waiver", "Only through the adviser's own trading account", "Direct plans with no commission"], 3, "Advisers implementing advice must use direct plans or commission-free routes, since they cannot earn distribution income. Using regular plans would breach segregation norms."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "A 52-year-old client with grown-up children and a paid-off home is most likely in which life-cycle stage?", ["Consolidation, nearing retirement", "Early accumulation, just starting a career", "Distribution, already drawing a pension for many years", "Dependence on parents for financial support"], 0, "In the consolidation stage, income is near its peak, major liabilities are largely settled and the focus shifts to building and protecting the retirement corpus. Distribution begins after retirement."], ["Derivatives", "A call option with strike ₹1,000 when the stock is at ₹1,080 is:", ["Out of the money", "At the money", "Worthless at any time", "In the money"], 3, "A call is in the money when the spot price exceeds the strike, since exercise would buy below market. Here intrinsic value is ₹80."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "Compared with buying commercial property directly, investing in a listed REIT offers:", ["Guaranteed rental income every month", "Smaller ticket size and better liquidity", "Complete control over the building's management", "No exposure to real estate market risk"], 1, "REIT units can be bought in small amounts and traded on exchanges, giving diversification across properties. Income is not guaranteed and unitholders still bear real estate risk."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "Which action is most likely to improve a client's credit score over time?", ["Applying for several new loans in the same month", "Using the full credit card limit every month", "Closing the oldest credit card and missing a payment", "Paying all EMIs and card dues on time"], 3, "Timely repayment is the biggest factor in credit scores. Multiple applications, high credit utilisation and missed payments all lower the score."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "In a core-satellite approach, the 'core' is usually invested in:", ["Low-cost, diversified funds", "Highly concentrated small-cap bets", "Frequent trading in options", "A single sector expected to boom"], 0, "The core holds broad, low-cost investments that capture market returns reliably. Satellites are smaller positions in active or thematic ideas that aim for extra returns."], ["Equity Investment", "Which risk of equity investing cannot be removed by holding many stocks across sectors?", ["Company-specific management risk", "Market risk", "Risk of a single factory fire", "Risk of one firm losing a key contract"], 1, "Systematic or market risk affects all stocks, for example from interest rates or economic slowdown, and cannot be diversified away. Events specific to one company are unsystematic and can be diversified."], ["Client Financial Position & Debt Management", "A client's monthly take-home income is ₹1,50,000 and her monthly expenses including EMIs are ₹1,05,000. What is her savings ratio?", ["30%", "70%", "45%", "35%"], 0, "Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,05,000 = ₹45,000. Savings ratio = 45,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = 30%. 70% is the share of income spent, not saved."], ["Derivatives", "A forward contract differs from a futures contract in that a forward is:", ["Traded on an exchange with daily margins", "Always settled in cash through a clearing corporation", "Available only on stock indices", "A private, customised OTC agreement"], 3, "Forwards are bilateral and customised, with counterparty risk and no daily margining. Futures are standardised, exchange-traded and cleared by a clearing corporation."], ["Securities Market Segments", "Online bond platform providers that sell listed debt securities to retail investors are regulated by:", ["IRDAI", "PFRDA", "SEBI", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 2, "SEBI regulates online bond platform providers, which must be registered as stock brokers in the debt segment and offer only listed or to-be-listed debt securities. IRDAI and PFRDA regulate insurance and pensions."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "A client changes his address. Updating it once with the KRA ensures that:", ["All linked intermediaries get the update", "Only his bank account is updated", "His PAN is cancelled and reissued", "His existing investments are redeemed"], 0, "KRAs share KYC data with registered intermediaries, so one update flows to mutual funds, brokers and others using that KYC. PAN and investments are unaffected."], ["Regulatory Framework for Investment Advisers", "Which of the following is an obligation of a registered investment adviser?", ["Guaranteeing returns on recommended products", "Maintaining records of advice and its basis", "Sharing client data with distributors", "Holding client funds in its own bank account"], 1, "Advisers must keep records of client profiles, advice given and the rationale for the prescribed period. They may not guarantee returns, share data improperly or handle client funds."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "Why should an investor add a nominee to a demat account?", ["To allow the nominee to trade freely now", "To reduce tax on dividends", "To ease transfer of holdings on death", "To increase the account's credit limit"], 2, "Nomination lets securities be transmitted smoothly to the nominee after the holder's death. It does not give the nominee trading rights during the holder's lifetime or affect tax."], ["Time Value of Money", "Compared with an ordinary annuity of the same amount, rate and term, the future value of an annuity due is:", ["Lower, because payments are made later", "Exactly the same in all cases", "Higher only when the interest rate is negative", "Higher, as each payment earns one extra period"], 3, "An annuity due has payments at the start of each period, so every payment compounds for one more period. Its value equals the ordinary annuity value multiplied by (1 + r)."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "A portfolio has 60% in asset A (standard deviation 20%) and 40% in asset B (standard deviation 8%), with a correlation of 0.2. Its standard deviation is closest to:", ["15.2%", "10.9%", "13.0%", "17.0%"], 2, "σp = √(0.6²×0.2² + 0.4²×0.08² + 2×0.6×0.4×0.2×0.08×0.2) ≈ 13.0%. 15.2% is the weighted average, which applies only with perfect correlation."], ["Portfolio Performance Evaluation", "An investment gave a return of 21% over two years. Its annualised (compound) return is closest to:", ["10.5%", "21.0%", "10.0%", "11.0%"], 2, "Annualised return = (1.21)^(1/2) − 1 = 10%. Dividing 21% by two gives 10.5%, which ignores compounding and overstates the yearly rate."], ["Investment Advisory & Financial Planning Basics", "Two clients have the same income and age. One is anxious about any fall in value; the other is comfortable with volatility. This difference relates mainly to their:", ["Risk capacity", "Investment horizon", "Liquidity needs", "Risk tolerance"], 3, "Risk tolerance is the psychological willingness to accept volatility. Risk capacity is the financial ability to bear losses, which here appears similar because income and age are the same."], ["Securities Market Segments", "Which entity guarantees settlement of trades executed on a stock exchange?", ["The depository participant", "The clearing corporation", "The registrar and transfer agent", "The issuing company"], 1, "The clearing corporation acts as central counterparty through novation and guarantees settlement. Depository participants hold demat accounts, RTAs maintain issuer records and the issuer has no role in trade settlement."], ["Indian Financial Markets", "Which of these is a function of financial intermediaries such as banks and mutual funds?", ["Printing currency notes for circulation", "Setting the policy repo rate", "Pooling savings and channelling them into investment", "Passing laws on taxation of income"], 2, "Intermediaries mobilise savings from many households and allocate them to borrowers and issuers, offering diversification and liquidity. Currency issue and policy rates are RBI functions; tax laws are made by Parliament."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "A client wants to convert physical share certificates into electronic form. This process is:", ["Dematerialisation", "Rematerialisation", "Transmission", "Pledging"], 0, "Dematerialisation converts physical certificates into electronic holdings through a DP. Rematerialisation is the reverse, transmission is transfer on death, and pledging creates a charge."], ["Operational Aspects (KYC, Demat, Transactions)", "The Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR) is maintained by:", ["SEBI", "CERSAI", "NSDL", "AMFI"], 1, "CKYCR is operated by the Central Registry of Securitisation Asset Reconstruction and Security Interest of India (CERSAI), allowing one KYC to be used across financial sectors. NSDL is a depository and AMFI is an industry body."], ["Time Value of Money", "What is the present value of ₹50,000 received at the end of each year for 10 years, discounted at 8% per annum?", ["₹5,00,000", "About ₹3,35,504", "About ₹3,62,344", "About ₹2,50,000"], 1, "PV of an ordinary annuity = 50,000 × (1 − 1.08^−10) ÷ 0.08 ≈ ₹3,35,504. ₹3,62,344 is the annuity-due value with payments at the start of each year, and ₹5,00,000 ignores discounting."], ["Asset Allocation & Portfolio Construction", "An investment policy statement (IPS) mainly records:", ["Daily market tips given to the client", "Objectives, constraints and allocation policy", "Only the adviser's fee invoice", "The client's tax return for the year"], 1, "The IPS documents the client's goals, risk tolerance, time horizon, liquidity and other constraints, target allocation and review rules. It guides decisions and keeps both parties disciplined."], ["Real Estate, Gold & Other Assets", "A key drawback of direct real estate as an investment is:", ["Its price is quoted every second on exchanges", "Illiquidity and high transaction costs", "It cannot be used as collateral for loans", "It always gives lower returns than bank deposits"], 1, "Property takes time to sell, needs large amounts and attracts stamp duty, registration and brokerage costs. These features reduce flexibility compared with financial assets."]]};
